1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,240
A language model generates text one

2
00:00:02,159 --> 00:00:06,240
token at a time and to produce each

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:09,280
token it reads every weight out of

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:11,360
memory. For a large model that is

5
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:14,400
hundreds of gigabytes crossing the bus

6
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:17,600
per token. We know by now where that

7
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:19,920
time goes. The arithmetic is over almost

8
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,560
instantly and the machine spends the

9
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:26,080
rest of the time waiting on memory. That

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:28,080
is the memory wall. In the last video,

11
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:29,840
every speed up came from changing the

12
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,760
route the bytes take through the

13
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:33,600
machine.

14
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:36,079
In this video, we shrink the bytes

15
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:38,399
themselves. Quantization stores each

16
00:00:36,079 --> 00:00:40,160
number in fewer bits and every bit you

17
00:00:38,399 --> 00:00:43,120
shave off the weights means gigabytes

18
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:45,440
that never cross the bus. The catch is

19
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:47,520
that it is lossy. Fewer bits means a

20
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,280
coarser approximation.

21
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:51,280
For the rest of the video, we'll work

22
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:53,840
out how to spend a tight bit budget

23
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:55,920
without breaking the model.

24
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:58,879
To spend that budget, well, we start

25
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:02,399
with what a number's bits actually do.

26
00:00:58,879 --> 00:01:05,600
Take scientific notation. 6.022

27
00:01:02,399 --> 00:01:08,120
* 10 23rd. It carries three pieces of

28
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:10,159
information. A sign, an exponent that

29
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:14,400
[music] says which power of 10 you sit

30
00:01:10,159 --> 00:01:16,479
in, and the significant, the 6.022.

31
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:19,759
A binary float keeps the same three

32
00:01:16,479 --> 00:01:22,560
fields in base 2. A sign bit, exponent

33
00:01:19,759 --> 00:01:25,680
bits and mantisa bits which hold the

34
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:28,080
significant. The exponent bits set the

35
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:30,720
dynamic range, how large or small a

36
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:33,040
value you can reach. The mantisa bits

37
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:36,479
set the precision, how finely you can

38
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:38,799
tell two nearby values apart.

39
00:01:36,479 --> 00:01:41,520
Because the exponent scales the value,

40
00:01:38,799 --> 00:01:44,240
floats are not evenly spaced. They crowd

41
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:46,560
near zero and spread apart as magnitudes

42
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:49,280
grow like the ticks on a logarithmic

43
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:51,439
ruler. And that turns out to be a

44
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:54,960
natural fit for the weights of a trained

45
00:01:51,439 --> 00:01:56,079
network which also crowd near zero.

46
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:58,000
Activations [music]

47
00:01:56,079 --> 00:02:00,960
the values that flow through the network

48
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,240
as it runs do not. And that mismatch is

49
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:07,280
most of what makes quantization hard.

50
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:10,080
FP32, the baseline, spends one bit on

51
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:13,040
the sign, eight on the exponent, and 23

52
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:16,000
on the mantisa. That buys a range near

53
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:18,720
10 to the plus or minus 38 and about 7

54
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,160
decimal digits of precision.

55
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:25,200
Every format below FP32 answers one

56
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:27,360
question. Which of those 32 bits can we

57
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,360
afford to throw away? Stack the answers

58
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:32,160
by size and we get a [music] ladder.

59
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:35,200
Each rung a tighter bit budget.

60
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:37,200
FP32 sits at the top. Full range, full

61
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:41,120
precision, four bytes each, too

62
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:43,840
expensive to move at scale. TF32 keeps

63
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:47,440
all eight exponent bits and trims the

64
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:51,920
mantisa to 10. It never reaches memory.

65
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:56,080
Inputs stay FP32, round to TF32 for the

66
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:58,879
multiply, then accumulate back in FP32.

67
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:02,480
on the A100 that bought roughly eight

68
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:06,560
times the single precision throughput.

69
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:09,840
FP16 and BF16 both spend 16 bits divided

70
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:13,519
in opposite ways. Below them sit the 8

71
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:15,680
bit floats then six and four bits. FP8

72
00:03:13,519 --> 00:03:18,720
splits into two variants the hardware

73
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:23,200
can mix in a single matrix multiply.

74
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:26,319
E4 M3 spends four bits on exponent and

75
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:29,040
three on mantisa. Less range, more

76
00:03:26,319 --> 00:03:31,200
precision for the forward pass weights

77
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:34,879
and activations.

78
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:36,959
E 5 M2 flips it five exponent and two

79
00:03:34,879 --> 00:03:39,920
mantisa for the wider range that

80
00:03:36,959 --> 00:03:44,400
backward pass gradients span. The

81
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:48,000
integers are the odd ones out. Int8 and

82
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:50,319
int4 carry no per value exponent. Just

83
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:53,760
evenly spaced steps [music] under one

84
00:03:50,319 --> 00:03:56,400
shared scale sized to the largest value.

85
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:58,480
That's simple and fast in silicon. But

86
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:00,959
wide-ranging values like activations

87
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:03,360
break it. One outlier stretches the

88
00:04:00,959 --> 00:04:06,159
scale and everything ordinary collapses

89
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:09,200
into the bottom few steps.

90
00:04:06,159 --> 00:04:12,080
Back to the two 16- bit formats.

91
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:14,400
FP16 spends 10 bits on the mantisa and

92
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:16,799
five on the exponent. So it separates

93
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:20,720
nearby values finely but reaches only a

94
00:04:16,799 --> 00:04:22,800
narrow window roughly 6 * 10 - 5 up to

95
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:25,360
about 65,000.

96
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:28,639
In a deep network, gradients slip below

97
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:30,560
that flow and underflow to exactly zero

98
00:04:28,639 --> 00:04:33,919
and a zero gradient teaches the model

99
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:36,720
nothing. FP16 training gets around this

100
00:04:33,919 --> 00:04:38,720
with loss scaling. Multiply the loss by

101
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:41,280
a large constant to lift the gradients

102
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:44,240
above the floor. Then divide it back out

103
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:45,520
before the weight update.

104
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:48,080
You might still [music] expect the

105
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:51,120
sharper format to win. For training,

106
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:55,280
range matters more than precision.

107
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:58,000
BF16 keeps [music] FP32's full 8bit

108
00:04:55,280 --> 00:05:01,360
exponent and spends just seven bits on

109
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,680
the mantisa. Gradients and activations

110
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:06,160
span many orders of magnitude. [music]

111
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:08,960
And what kills training is overflow or

112
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:10,560
underflow. Small rounding errors barely

113
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:14,400
matter.

114
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:17,360
So BF16 rarely needs loss scaling and it

115
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:19,600
became the default training format.

116
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:21,680
That is really a training story. At

117
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:23,840
inference there are no gradients. So the

118
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:26,639
underflow that forces loss scaling never

119
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:28,400
happens. And FP16 is a common serving

120
00:05:26,639 --> 00:05:30,479
format.

121
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:33,840
That ladder hands us plenty of small

122
00:05:30,479 --> 00:05:35,840
formats. The actual question is why a

123
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:38,800
trained network survives being squeezed

124
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:41,759
into them at all. The answer is in the

125
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:44,160
weights themselves. A trained network is

126
00:05:41,759 --> 00:05:46,160
overparameterized. No single weight

127
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:47,759
carries much information and rounding

128
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:49,280
nudges each one up or down

129
00:05:47,759 --> 00:05:51,919
independently.

130
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:54,720
Inside the network's long weighted sums,

131
00:05:51,919 --> 00:05:57,120
those nudges mostly cancel. Had they all

132
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:59,520
pushed one way, they would pile up and

133
00:05:57,120 --> 00:06:01,520
break the model.

134
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:03,680
The weights also cluster tightly around

135
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:07,360
zero and a tight cluster is easy to

136
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:10,000
cover. Even in T4 with just 16 distinct

137
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:12,720
values to spend lands nearly all of them

138
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,120
where the weights live.

139
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:16,720
Now take those same 16 values to the

140
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:19,600
activations.

141
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:22,319
Most cluster near 02, but a few run

142
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:24,400
around 100 times larger and the format's

143
00:06:22,319 --> 00:06:26,880
single shared scale must stretch to

144
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:28,639
reach them.

145
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:30,240
These extremes are called outlier

146
00:06:28,639 --> 00:06:32,639
features.

147
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:35,280
Detm and his colleagues mapped them in

148
00:06:32,639 --> 00:06:38,400
the LLM [music] int 8 paper. As a

149
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:40,160
transformer grows, a few channels, the

150
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:42,720
same positions in every token's

151
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:45,280
activation vector, start carrying values

152
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:47,759
far larger than the rest. They appear

153
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:51,440
abruptly in what the paper calls a phase

154
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:53,199
shift at about 6.7 billion parameters.

155
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:55,039
And from there, they show up in every

156
00:06:53,199 --> 00:06:57,440
layer.

157
00:06:55,039 --> 00:07:01,280
At that scale, with a sequence length of

158
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:03,759
248, the paper counts roughly 150,000

159
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:06,160
outliers per sequence, concentrated in

160
00:07:03,759 --> 00:07:09,120
just six of those channels, about a

161
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:11,919
tenth of a percent of all features.

162
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:14,400
Yet, zero those six channels out and the

163
00:07:11,919 --> 00:07:17,360
model falls apart. The top token's share

164
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:19,759
of attention drops by more than 20% and

165
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:22,800
perplexity, the standard measure of how

166
00:07:19,759 --> 00:07:26,080
well a model predicts text, worsens by

167
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:28,000
600 to a,000%.

168
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:30,720
The damage they do to quantization is

169
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,039
mechanical. One scale must cover the

170
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:35,759
largest value in its group. So a channel

171
00:07:33,039 --> 00:07:38,240
running say 100 times hotter stretches

172
00:07:35,759 --> 00:07:40,639
the scale wide and every normal value

173
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:44,400
collapses into the bottom few steps

174
00:07:40,639 --> 00:07:46,800
where real information turns to noise.

175
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:48,479
From here every method and every format

176
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,560
is a different way of handling these

177
00:07:48,479 --> 00:07:52,720
outliers.

178
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,560
Which of those methods helps depends on

179
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:56,160
what the hardware is waiting on. [music]

180
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:58,639
And we already have the diagram for

181
00:07:56,160 --> 00:08:00,160
that. The roof line from the first

182
00:07:58,639 --> 00:08:02,479
video.

183
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:05,440
We plot attainable performance against

184
00:08:02,479 --> 00:08:08,479
arithmetic intensity. The operations a

185
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:10,240
workload gets done per bite it moves.

186
00:08:08,479 --> 00:08:12,160
The curve climbs along the bandwidth

187
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:14,160
limit then flattens at the compute

188
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:16,400
ceiling. [music] They meet at the ridge

189
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:18,560
just under 300 operations per bite on

190
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:21,120
the H100.

191
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:23,759
Left of the ridge you are memory bound.

192
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:27,120
Math units idling while data trickles

193
00:08:23,759 --> 00:08:28,879
in. Right of it computebound. The units

194
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:30,800
saturated.

195
00:08:28,879 --> 00:08:33,200
Quantisation pushes in exactly one

196
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:35,519
direction on this diagram. Fewer bytes

197
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:38,240
per operation means higher intensity. So

198
00:08:35,519 --> 00:08:40,560
the workload slides right. While it

199
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:44,080
stays left of the ridge, that slide is

200
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:46,959
pure speed up. Cross the ridge and fewer

201
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:49,440
bites by almost nothing.

202
00:08:46,959 --> 00:08:52,720
Inference has two phases and they land

203
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:55,279
on opposite sides of that ridge. Prefill

204
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:57,760
processes the whole prompt at once. big

205
00:08:55,279 --> 00:09:00,160
matrix multiplies and every weight it

206
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:03,200
loads is reused across hundreds of

207
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:06,800
tokens in parallel. Arithmetic intensity

208
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:10,480
is high. The phase runs computebound and

209
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:13,600
on an H100 tensor core utilization can

210
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:16,101
push past 90%.

211
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:16,640
Decode generates one token at a time.

212
00:09:16,101 --> 00:09:18,959
[music]

213
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:21,519
Each step is a skinny matrix vector

214
00:09:18,959 --> 00:09:24,160
multiply that rereads all the weights

215
00:09:21,519 --> 00:09:27,200
and the whole KV cache. The attention

216
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:30,480
state saved for every pass token to emit

217
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:32,959
a single token that is memory bound and

218
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:35,440
utilization on the same GPU can crater

219
00:09:32,959 --> 00:09:38,240
to 20 to 40%.

220
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:41,440
The model and the GPU are identical in

221
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:43,279
both phases. All that changes is how

222
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:45,519
many tokens share each load of the

223
00:09:43,279 --> 00:09:46,959
weights and the bottleneck flips with

224
00:09:45,519 --> 00:09:48,800
it.

225
00:09:46,959 --> 00:09:51,440
Two bottlenecks call for two

226
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:55,360
quantization strategies.

227
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:58,480
Weighton quantization written W4 A16 for

228
00:09:55,360 --> 00:10:01,200
4-bit weights with 16 bit activations

229
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:03,680
shrinks only the weights that is aimed

230
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:06,560
squarely at memory bound decode. Each

231
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:09,440
token pulls fewer bytes from HPM. The

232
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:11,279
high bandwidth memory beside the GPU die

233
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:14,800
even though the math itself still runs

234
00:10:11,279 --> 00:10:16,560
in 16 bits. The win is bandwidth not

235
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:20,399
arithmetic

236
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:24,320
weight and activation quantization W8 A8

237
00:10:20,399 --> 00:10:26,560
or W4 A4 puts both sides of the multiply

238
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:28,880
in low precision and that unlocks

239
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:31,760
something else entirely. The tensor

240
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:35,279
cores low precision math where having

241
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:37,360
the bits roughly doubles the throughput.

242
00:10:35,279 --> 00:10:40,000
That is what speeds up computebound

243
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:42,320
prefill and decode. Once the batch grows

244
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:45,200
large enough that many tokens again

245
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:47,440
share each weight load, the choice

246
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:51,440
follows the phase. Shrink the weights

247
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:54,320
for decode. Shrink both for prefill.

248
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:56,640
That covers when quantization pays off.

249
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:58,720
Now for the methods themselves and each

250
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:03,040
one deals with the outliers in its own

251
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:06,160
way. GPTQ from Frontar and colleagues in

252
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:08,720
2022 quantizes a layer's weights one at

253
00:11:06,160 --> 00:11:10,959
a time. After rounding each weight, it

254
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:13,760
nudges everything not yet quantized to

255
00:11:10,959 --> 00:11:16,240
cancel the error just introduced.

256
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:18,160
How much to nudge comes from the Hessian

257
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:20,160
second order information that measures

258
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:23,040
how much each weights error moves the

259
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:25,120
layer's output. For a linear layer, that

260
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:27,839
is just a statistic of the inputs

261
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:30,240
collected by running calibration data. a

262
00:11:27,839 --> 00:11:33,200
small sample of real inputs through the

263
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:35,120
model. An error on a weight matters

264
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:36,880
exactly as much as the input it

265
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:39,760
multiplies.

266
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:42,320
Notice what GPTQ never does. It never

267
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:44,079
treats the outliers as special. It

268
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:46,320
spreads the error they cause across the

269
00:11:44,079 --> 00:11:48,240
remaining weights wherever the output is

270
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:50,000
least sensitive.

271
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:52,640
That was enough for three and four bits

272
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:55,600
in one shot. No retraining at near

273
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:57,839
baseline accuracy and it fit a 175

274
00:11:55,600 --> 00:12:00,480
billion parameter model onto a single

275
00:11:57,839 --> 00:12:02,800
GPU for the first time.

276
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:05,680
Smooth quant from Shiao and colleagues

277
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:08,880
at MIT attacks the outliers by moving

278
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:12,320
them. Weights are easy, activations are

279
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:14,079
hard. So it applies a rescaling that is

280
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:16,560
mathematically equivalent to doing

281
00:12:14,079 --> 00:12:19,279
nothing. Divide each activation channel

282
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:21,680
by a factor S. Multiply the matching

283
00:12:19,279 --> 00:12:24,639
weight channel by the same S. The

284
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:26,639
product is unchanged, but choose S well,

285
00:12:24,639 --> 00:12:28,160
and the outliers shrink while the

286
00:12:26,639 --> 00:12:30,639
weights absorb a little of the

287
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:32,160
difficulty until both sides quantize

288
00:12:30,639 --> 00:12:36,160
comfortably.

289
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:40,160
One knob alpha, usually a half, sets how

290
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:43,360
much to shift. That is enough for W8 A8

291
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:46,880
with little accuracy loss.

292
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:49,680
AWQ from the same MIT group works the

293
00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:51,920
weight side instead. The 1% of weight

294
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:53,680
channels that matter most found by the

295
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:56,480
size of the activations flowing through

296
00:12:53,680 --> 00:12:59,200
them get scaled up so rounding barely

297
00:12:56,480 --> 00:13:01,120
touches them and plain 4bit weight only

298
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:03,680
stays accurate.

299
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:06,399
Quarrot and spin quant reach for the

300
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:09,760
trick flash attention three used in the

301
00:13:06,399 --> 00:13:11,600
last video. Rotate. An orthogonal

302
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:14,399
rotation of weights and activations

303
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:17,040
together leaves every output unchanged

304
00:13:14,399 --> 00:13:19,279
but smears each outlier thinly across

305
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:22,079
all the channels so no single one

306
00:13:19,279 --> 00:13:25,360
stretches the scale. Quarro's rotation

307
00:13:22,079 --> 00:13:29,200
is a fixed pattern cheap to apply on the

308
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:31,519
fly. Spin quant from meta learns its

309
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:34,079
rotation from data.

310
00:13:31,519 --> 00:13:36,639
Rotation is what finally makes 4bit

311
00:13:34,079 --> 00:13:39,279
activations workable.

312
00:13:36,639 --> 00:13:41,920
Decode's other memory hog is the KV

313
00:13:39,279 --> 00:13:45,600
cache. The keys and values attention

314
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:48,240
saves for every pass token. It grows

315
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:50,399
with sequence length times batch size.

316
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:53,040
And at long context, it overtakes the

317
00:13:50,399 --> 00:13:56,160
weights both to fit in memory and to

318
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:58,160
reread on every token.

319
00:13:56,160 --> 00:14:00,880
Quantizing it is harder than quantizing

320
00:13:58,160 --> 00:14:03,760
weights because weights sit still. Study

321
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:05,680
them once, quantize them offline. The

322
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:08,160
cache does not exist until a request

323
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:11,120
arrives and its values change with every

324
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:13,600
input. Worse, the keys carry their own

325
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:15,680
per channel outlier structure. A few

326
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:17,760
fixed channels that run large no matter

327
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:20,240
the input.

328
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:22,480
Kivy [music] and KV quant two groups

329
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:24,560
working independently landed on the same

330
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:27,120
recipe. A scale per key channel to

331
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:29,199
corral the outliers and per token scales

332
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:30,720
for the values which lack that channel

333
00:14:29,199 --> 00:14:33,839
structure.

334
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:36,560
and errors compound, a badly quantized

335
00:14:33,839 --> 00:14:39,120
early token leaks into every later

336
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:42,399
token's attention.

337
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:44,639
The architectural alternative is MLA

338
00:14:42,399 --> 00:14:47,199
from Deepseek, which we unpacked in the

339
00:14:44,639 --> 00:14:50,000
kernel's video. Instead of quantizing

340
00:14:47,199 --> 00:14:53,120
the cache, it caches a small learned

341
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:56,160
summary, the latent, and rebuilds keys

342
00:14:53,120 --> 00:14:59,279
and values from it on the fly. Deepseek

343
00:14:56,160 --> 00:15:01,440
reports the cache shrinks 93.3%

344
00:14:59,279 --> 00:15:05,440
and maximum generation throughput rises

345
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:08,399
5.76 times over their previous model.

346
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:11,519
One approach shrinks every cache number.

347
00:15:08,399 --> 00:15:14,079
The other caches fewer numbers.

348
00:15:11,519 --> 00:15:17,120
Underneath all of these methods sits a

349
00:15:14,079 --> 00:15:20,399
simpler choice. How many numbers share a

350
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:22,959
single scale? At one extreme is per

351
00:15:20,399 --> 00:15:25,440
tensor scaling. one scale for an entire

352
00:15:22,959 --> 00:15:28,000
weight or activation matrix, cheapest

353
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:30,000
and most fragile because one outlier

354
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:32,880
anywhere forces that scale wide for

355
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,800
everything. Per channel and per token

356
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:38,320
scaling the grain smooth quant and the

357
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:40,639
KV cache methods work at are finer.

358
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:43,279
Microscaling pushes to the finest grain

359
00:15:40,639 --> 00:15:46,720
that is still practical. A shared scale

360
00:15:43,279 --> 00:15:49,839
for every small block of 16 or 32 values

361
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:52,959
stored right alongside them. This is an

362
00:15:49,839 --> 00:15:55,759
old idea called block floating point and

363
00:15:52,959 --> 00:15:58,240
its payoff is containment. A local

364
00:15:55,759 --> 00:16:00,959
outlier blows up its own little block

365
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:04,560
and nothing else.

366
00:16:00,959 --> 00:16:08,399
Two 4bit formats compete at that grain.

367
00:16:04,560 --> 00:16:12,000
MX FP4 the open compute standard packs

368
00:16:08,399 --> 00:16:15,360
FP4 values, two exponent bits and one

369
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:18,720
mantisa into blocks of 32. Each block

370
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:21,600
shares an E8 M0 scale, eight exponent

371
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:23,839
bits and no mantisa, a pure power of two

372
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:26,639
that is cheap to apply as a bit shift

373
00:16:23,839 --> 00:16:29,680
but coarse off by up to a full factor of

374
00:16:26,639 --> 00:16:32,320
two. NVFP4

375
00:16:29,680 --> 00:16:35,759
Nvidia's Blackwell format tightens both

376
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:38,320
choices. Its blocks drop to 16, each

377
00:16:35,759 --> 00:16:41,440
scaled by an FP8 value that can land

378
00:16:38,320 --> 00:16:44,399
between powers of two with one FP32

379
00:16:41,440 --> 00:16:46,079
scale over the whole tensor. Nvidia

380
00:16:44,399 --> 00:16:49,519
reports that combination cuts

381
00:16:46,079 --> 00:16:51,759
quantization error by 88% compared to

382
00:16:49,519 --> 00:16:53,360
MXFP4.

383
00:16:51,759 --> 00:16:55,360
You would guess the biggest models are

384
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:57,759
the hardest to squeeze into four bits.

385
00:16:55,360 --> 00:17:00,240
It is the other way around.

386
00:16:57,759 --> 00:17:03,600
On the largest models, hundreds of

387
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:06,640
billions of parameters, NVFP4

388
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:08,160
lands within about one accuracy point of

389
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:10,079
BF16

390
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:12,720
or FP8.

391
00:17:10,079 --> 00:17:14,720
On smaller models under about 50 billion

392
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:17,600
parameters, plain post-training

393
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:20,079
quantization, no retraining can give up

394
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:22,000
four to 14 points on hard reasoning

395
00:17:20,079 --> 00:17:24,559
benchmarks.

396
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:27,520
Near lossless four bits is a large model

397
00:17:24,559 --> 00:17:29,840
story. The usual explanation is that big

398
00:17:27,520 --> 00:17:32,799
models carry enough redundancy to absorb

399
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:35,200
the rounding. small ones do not and need

400
00:17:32,799 --> 00:17:37,840
quantization aware training where the

401
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:40,720
model trains with the rounding baked in

402
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:42,799
or a few sensitive layers left in higher

403
00:17:40,720 --> 00:17:45,200
precision.

404
00:17:42,799 --> 00:17:47,600
Two cautions though that redundancy

405
00:17:45,200 --> 00:17:50,480
explanation is plausible but nobody has

406
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:52,480
measured it directly and many of these

407
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:54,960
4-bit numbers come from Nvidia's own

408
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:57,200
benchmarks and independent reproductions

409
00:17:54,960 --> 00:18:00,160
are still scarce.

410
00:17:57,200 --> 00:18:02,720
So how do you actually choose? Start by

411
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:05,200
profiling your own bottleneck. If HPM

412
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:07,840
bandwidth runs above 80% on your hot

413
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:11,200
path, you are memory bound. Optimize

414
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:13,679
bytes, not math. For pure memory

415
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:19,440
savings, the default is 4bit weight

416
00:18:13,679 --> 00:18:22,640
only. AWQ or GPTQ at W4 A16. [music]

417
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:25,440
The safest, most widely deployed win,

418
00:18:22,640 --> 00:18:29,039
roughly four times smaller than FP16 and

419
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:31,679
faster decode on almost any GPU.

420
00:18:29,039 --> 00:18:34,400
For computebound speedups, match the

421
00:18:31,679 --> 00:18:37,120
format to the silicon. Smooth quant

422
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:39,440
style FB8 is the proven recipe, but it

423
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:43,039
only pays off on Hopper or newer and

424
00:18:39,440 --> 00:18:45,600
NVFP4 only on Blackwell. On older chips,

425
00:18:43,039 --> 00:18:48,960
low precision math buys nothing. So stay

426
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:51,360
weight only. If you push activations all

427
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:55,280
the way to four bits, that is rotation

428
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:58,160
territory, quarrot or spin quant. And at

429
00:18:55,280 --> 00:19:00,799
long context, once the KV cache outgrows

430
00:18:58,160 --> 00:19:03,840
the weights, quantize the cache per

431
00:19:00,799 --> 00:19:06,960
channel for keys per token for values or

432
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:09,760
move to an MLA style architecture.

433
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:11,360
Below about 70 billion parameters, do

434
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:13,919
not ship 4 bits straight from

435
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:15,840
post-training quantization. Budget for

436
00:19:13,919 --> 00:19:17,600
quantization aware training or

437
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:20,480
distillation from the full precision

438
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:22,400
model and keep the sensitive layers in

439
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:24,160
higher precision.

440
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:25,679
Above all, measure on your own

441
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:27,919
benchmarks.

442
00:19:25,679 --> 00:19:30,960
Published near lossless numbers are

443
00:19:27,919 --> 00:19:33,760
model specific, benchmark specific, and

444
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:36,799
often vendor sourced. Treat them as

445
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:39,200
upper bounds, not guarantees.

446
00:19:36,799 --> 00:19:41,840
Back to the bit budget.

447
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:44,160
Quantization spends it well. Fewer bits

448
00:19:41,840 --> 00:19:46,880
per number, fewer bytes across the

449
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:50,320
memory wall, and every format and method

450
00:19:46,880 --> 00:19:53,120
we met was one way or another a plan for

451
00:19:50,320 --> 00:19:56,080
handling the outliers.

452
00:19:53,120 --> 00:19:58,480
Two limits remain. Even at four bits,

453
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:01,120
the largest models do not fit on one

454
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:03,520
chip. A multi- trillion parameter model

455
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:05,840
is still measured in terabytes. So, it

456
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:08,640
must be split across many chips and you

457
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:11,280
pay for the communication between them.

458
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:13,200
And there is a subtler limit for any

459
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:16,160
given token. Most of the weights

460
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:18,960
contribute almost nothing.

461
00:20:16,160 --> 00:20:21,760
Each limit has its own answer.

462
00:20:18,960 --> 00:20:23,280
Parallelism splits one model across many

463
00:20:21,760 --> 00:20:25,440
chips.

464
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:28,159
Mixture of experts lets [music] each

465
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:29,760
token activate only a small slice of the

466
00:20:28,159 --> 00:20:32,320
network.

467
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:34,720
One caveat to carry forward. Memory

468
00:20:32,320 --> 00:20:37,440
savings from fewer bits are reliable on

469
00:20:34,720 --> 00:20:40,880
